{%- comment -%}
  The making, retitled from cloth to construction.

  This section used to run Spin / Weave / Finish, which told the cloth
  story a second time: lada-promise already sets out what our own looms
  buy you, and lada-collection sells the cloth line on every card. A
  customer looking at a coat priced above the rest of the shelf is not
  asking where the yarn came from, he is asking who cut it and who sat
  at the machine. So the three stages are Cut / Sew / Finish, and the
  copy is the pattern and the chalk, the machinist and the seam, the
  press and the lining.

  THE NO-IMAGE STATE IS THE STATE THIS SHIPS IN. There is exactly one
  Lada photograph on the site and it is the hero plate. The template
  was pointing these three blocks at men-unstitched.jpg and
  men-shawls.jpg, which are borrowed cloth shots standing in for
  workshop frames. A picture of a folded bolt above the word "Sew"
  argues against the copy it sits on. Those stand-ins are cleared, so
  every stage renders image-free until the floor is actually shot.
  Two consequences are deliberate:

    1. Nothing reserves a 4:5 box that has no picture in it. An empty
       slot under a hairline reads as an image that failed to load,
       which is worse than no image at all. The figure is skipped, the
       column collapses to type, and .lada__stages--nomedia gives the
       triptych more air so it reads as set type rather than as a
       gallery with the pictures missing.
    2. Columns align to the top, not to a shared media baseline, so a
       half-filled state (one frame shot, two not) still looks like a
       decision instead of a layout error.
    3. The grid container itself is gated on there being a stage to
       put in it. .lada__stages carries margin-top:var(--s7), so a
       section with no blocks, or with blocks the client has added
       and not yet typed into, otherwise held three rems of dead air
       between the intro and the owner's line with nothing in it. An
       empty grid is the same failure as an empty image box: it reads
       as content that did not arrive.

  The owner's line is real italic. Montserrat is self-hosted in
  fonts.css as a variable face at 100-900 in BOTH styles, so
  font-style:italic at weight 300 renders as drawn and is not a
  browser-synthesised slant. It is gated on the note itself: with the
  setting empty the rule, the quote and the signature all disappear
  together, because a signature with nothing above it is just a name.

  The karigar credit is per block and has no default on purpose. Naming
  a man on a public page is his call, not ours; the line stays hidden
  until someone has actually asked him.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- liquid
  # media_count picks the spacing, stage_count decides whether the
  # grid is emitted at all. They are not the same number: a stage can
  # be filled with type and carry no photograph, which is the state
  # this page ships in.
  assign media_count = 0
  assign stage_count = 0
  for block in section.blocks
    assign filled = false
    if block.settings.image != blank or block.settings.asset_file != blank
      assign media_count = media_count | plus: 1
      assign filled = true
    endif
    if block.settings.kicker != blank or block.settings.title != blank or block.settings.text != blank
      assign filled = true
    endif
    if filled
      assign stage_count = stage_count | plus: 1
    endif
  endfor

  assign stages_class = 'lada__stages'
  if media_count == 0
    assign stages_class = 'lada__stages lada__stages--nomedia'
  endif
-%}
<section class="lada" id="lada-craft" aria-labelledby="lada-craft-h">
  <div class="lada__wrap lada__sec">
    <p class="lada__kicker">{{ section.settings.kicker | default: 'The making' }}</p>
    <h2 class="lada__h2" id="lada-craft-h">{{ section.settings.heading | default: 'Three tables, one coat' }}</h2>
    {%- if section.settings.intro != blank -%}<p class="lada__p">{{ section.settings.intro }}</p>{%- endif -%}

    {%- if stage_count > 0 -%}
    <div class="{{ stages_class }}">
      {%- assign stage_n = 0 -%}
      {%- for block in section.blocks -%}
        {%- comment -%}
          A block the client has added but not yet typed into is not a
          stage. Skipping it here rather than printing a bare hairline
          with a dangling numeral under it is what keeps a half-filled
          triptych looking edited. The numeral comes off its own
          counter for the same reason: forloop.index would count the
          skipped block and hand the reader 01 and 03.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- liquid
          assign has_media = false
          if block.settings.image != blank or block.settings.asset_file != blank
            assign has_media = true
          endif
          unless has_media or block.settings.kicker != blank or block.settings.title != blank or block.settings.text != blank
            continue
          endunless
          assign stage_n = stage_n | plus: 1
        -%}
        <article class="lada__stage" {{ block.shopify_attributes }}>
          {%- comment -%}
            Alt text falls back down a chain rather than repeating the
            title, which on a three-stage grid would give a screen
            reader "Cut. The pattern and the chalk. The pattern and the
            chalk." The per-block field wins, because it is the only
            one of the three a client can reach from this section and
            because the asset route carries no alt of its own; the
            picked image's alt from Files is next; a described sentence
            is the last resort rather than alt="", because these are
            content photographs, not ornament.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          {%- capture alt_fallback -%}The {{ block.settings.kicker | downcase }} stage at the Khan Jee workshop in Sheikhupura{%- endcapture -%}
          {%- assign stage_alt = block.settings.image_alt | default: block.settings.image.alt | default: alt_fallback -%}

          {%- if block.settings.image != blank -%}
            {%- comment -%}
              Never ask Shopify for more pixels than the file has: the
              cap is the source width, and the two smaller steps are
              only emitted once the source can actually supply them.
              Width and height are the real intrinsic values so the
              4:5 box is reserved before the bytes arrive.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            {%- assign iw = block.settings.image.width -%}
            {%- if iw > 1100 %}{% assign iw = 1100 %}{% endif -%}
            {%- capture stage_srcset -%}
              {%- if iw > 500 -%}{{ block.settings.image | image_url: width: 500 }} 500w, {% endif -%}
              {%- if iw > 800 -%}{{ block.settings.image | image_url: width: 800 }} 800w, {% endif -%}
              {{- block.settings.image | image_url: width: iw }} {{ iw }}w
            {%- endcapture -%}
            <img src="{{ block.settings.image | image_url: width: iw }}"
                 srcset="{{ stage_srcset | strip_newlines | strip }}"
                 sizes="(min-width:820px) 30vw, calc(100vw - 2.5rem)"
                 width="{{ block.settings.image.width }}" height="{{ block.settings.image.height }}"
                 loading="lazy" decoding="async"
                 alt="{{ stage_alt | escape }}">
          {%- elsif block.settings.asset_file != blank -%}
            {%- comment -%}
              A theme asset reports no dimensions, so the 4:5 box the
              CSS enforces is stated here instead. Anything dropped in
              this slot should be cropped 4:5 before upload.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            <img src="{{ block.settings.asset_file | asset_url }}"
                 width="900" height="1125" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
                 alt="{{ stage_alt | escape }}">
          {%- endif -%}

          {%- comment -%}
            "01 · CUT". The numeral is typed as a numeral and the stage
            name is typed in sentence case; the capitals are
            text-transform in CSS, so nothing in the theme editor has
            to be shouted at.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          <p class="n">
            {{- stage_n | prepend: '0' -}}
            {%- if block.settings.kicker != blank %} &middot; {{ block.settings.kicker }}{% endif -%}
          </p>
          {%- if block.settings.title != blank -%}<h3>{{ block.settings.title }}</h3>{%- endif -%}
          {%- if block.settings.text != blank -%}<p>{{ block.settings.text }}</p>{%- endif -%}
          {%- if block.settings.credit != blank -%}
            <p class="lada__stage-credit">{{ block.settings.credit }}</p>
          {%- endif -%}
        </article>
      {%- endfor -%}
    </div>
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- if section.settings.owner_note != blank -%}
      <figure class="lada__craft-note">
        <blockquote class="lada__craft-say">{{ section.settings.owner_note }}</blockquote>
        {%- if section.settings.owner_name != blank -%}
          <figcaption class="lada__craft-sig">
            {{- section.settings.owner_name -}}
            {%- if section.settings.owner_role != blank %}, {{ section.settings.owner_role }}{% endif -%}
          </figcaption>
        {%- endif -%}
      </figure>
    {%- endif -%}
  </div>
</section>

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "Lada craft",
  "tag": "section",
  "settings": [
    { "type": "text", "id": "kicker", "label": "Kicker", "default": "The making" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "heading", "label": "Heading", "default": "Three tables, one coat" },
    { "type": "textarea", "id": "intro", "label": "Intro", "default": "A coat is made three times before anyone wears it: once in chalk, once under the needle, once under the iron. All three happen at our own unit in Sheikhupura, on tables we can walk to." },
    { "type": "header", "content": "The owner's line" },
    { "type": "paragraph", "content": "One first-person sentence or two, signed. The whole block, rule and signature included, is hidden while the note is empty." },
    { "type": "textarea", "id": "owner_note", "label": "Note", "default": "I mark the first pattern for every new block myself, and no coat leaves this floor before I have put the shoulder on the stand. If it sits wrong when it reaches you, it comes back to my table, not to a form." },
    { "type": "text", "id": "owner_name", "label": "Signed", "default": "Abdul Hayee Khan" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "owner_role", "label": "Role or place", "info": "Optional. Appended after the name. Hidden until filled." }
  ],
  "blocks": [
    { "type": "stage", "name": "Stage", "settings": [
      { "type": "paragraph", "content": "A workshop frame, cropped 4:5. No model, no finished garment. The stage renders as type alone until an image is supplied, so leaving this empty is a valid state." },
      { "type": "image_picker", "id": "image", "label": "Image" },
      { "type": "text", "id": "asset_file", "label": "…or theme asset filename" },
      { "type": "text", "id": "image_alt", "label": "Alt text", "info": "Describe the work in the frame. Used with the asset filename, or when the picked image has no alt of its own." },
      { "type": "text", "id": "kicker", "label": "Stage", "default": "Cut", "info": "Set in capitals by the stylesheet. Type it in sentence case." },
      { "type": "text", "id": "title", "label": "Title", "default": "The pattern and the chalk" },
      { "type": "textarea", "id": "text", "label": "Text", "default": "Describe what happens at this table, in the order it happens, and name the thing a customer would not otherwise know." },
      { "type": "text", "id": "credit", "label": "Karigar credit", "info": "Optional, and only with his consent. For example: Cut by Muhammad Aslam, nineteen years at this table. Hidden until filled." }
    ]}
  ],
  "max_blocks": 3,
  "presets": [
    {
      "name": "Lada craft",
      "blocks": [
        { "type": "stage", "settings": {
          "kicker": "Cut",
          "title": "The pattern and the chalk",
          "text": "Your measurements become a paper pattern, and that pattern is chalked onto the cloth by hand. Stripe and check are matched across the two fronts before the blade moves, because a join that misses by a hair is visible for the life of the coat."
        }},
        { "type": "stage", "settings": {
          "kicker": "Sew",
          "title": "The machinist and the seam",
          "text": "One machinist takes a coat from the first seam to the last, so the tension never changes hands. The armholes and the collar stand are set by hand, and the shoulder goes on the stand to be looked at before the lining comes near it."
        }},
        { "type": "stage", "settings": {
          "kicker": "Finish",
          "title": "The press and the lining",
          "text": "The lining is set in by hand, the pocket mouths are bar tacked, and the coat is pressed in stages over a ham rather than flattened in one pass. Buttons go on last, sewn with a shank so they sit proud of the cloth and stay where they are put."
        }}
      ]
    }
  ]
}
{% endschema %}
